Data-to-text generation has a broad range of practical applications, including report generation, automated journalism, data visualization, and dialogue systems. In these applications, the input can consist of various types of data, such as tables, graphs, or raw data. It is worth noting that data-to-text generation is a controlled form of text generation, where the output must be coherent with the input and maintain semantic accuracy as a crucial aspect.

One popular approach for data-to-text generation involves fine-tuning pre-trained models, such as T5 \cite{10.5555/3455716.3455856}, on specific training data designed for data-to-text generation \cite{kale-rastogi-2020-text,yin-wan-2022-seq2seq}. This strategy has shown promising performance and can even surpass the capabilities of general-purpose large language models like ChatGPT or GPT-4 (for more details, refer to \hyperref[sec:chatgpt]{Comparing to LLMs}). Although current methods generally produce correct and fluent natural language outputs, they may occasionally suffer from semantic errors, particularly omissions that result in the absence of crucial keywords \cite{yin-wan-2022-seq2seq}. In this paper, we introduce the 'slot error rate (SER),' which quantifies the rate of missing keywords.


